erested that you think i if i'm a  restaurant owner  and i'm allowed now to serve meals  indoors  uh that if i say but to do that i want  to see your  vaccine passport that that i won't be  litigated to hell  i mean in the most litigious country in  the world i think you've got more  lawyers in the rest of the world  put together i mean this is not a  dripping roast for lawyers  it is no matter what you know if you've  got something that's got a toxic  combination of being  highly controversial and polite and  political  um and also you've got a lot of lawyers  running around you will get  litigation um you know certainly  um i think one thing is absolutely clear  that businesses  can require proof of vaccination of  their employees  i think you know we've had federal  guidance on that it already happens  um you know with hospitals on flu at  universities with various  um uh uh vaccination requirements  at schools i think we you know we are  going to see  um these this kind of thing happening  for customers i also think it will  happen it's a little bit of a gray zone  but i don't think much i mean there's  this  you'll see signs on a lot of american  shops  you might even see them in the uk you  know no shirt  no shoes no service sure you actually  absolutely are allowed to set standards  about your hygiene and health and safety  standards for those who you serve  we already require masks you know  even in states that don't mandate masks  businesses say you know you can't shop  here or you can't dine here  unless you put on a mask same thing with  a vaccine  you know a vaccine gives you a certain  cushion of safety and i think it's in  the economic  interests of businesses  to make their employees and their  customers feel  safe and i don't think there's anything  wrong with that  you know there's this huge argument in  the united states i'm sure there is in  the uk  and i lived there for 15 years and my  wife's from the lake district  so i know it really well you 