e the spring and summer months  ahead  that you sounded quite optimistic about  america's position overall  as we approached the end of this year  did did i pick that up correctly  you did you know and i'm and and those  who followed  my what i've said publicly um  i i've always been um you know very  um dour and pessimistic about this  and you know you know when it when it  after it arose in wuhan and and came to  europe and the u.s  i said this would take about two years  before we came out of it  just historically that's what pandemics  are like they have  two year cycles and we're in a period of  of crop  quite advanced science and people  thought that i was being  awfully um pessimistic and i've been  pessimistic throughout but uh  one thing i do know that in the uk  europe  the united states for some reason  we have the virus has been just too much  for us to handle  we either won't or can't change our  behavior  um the way that other countries have  been able to do um but one thing we're  really good at particularly in the uk in  the u.s  is science and and vaccine development  and  deployment that's what's going to get us  out of this and so yes  um by the autumn and certainly by the  end of this year  i i think we are going to be approaching  we're not going to be  completely normal we will be approaching  more normalcy  um we'll probably still quite a while be  masking and distancing  um but i do think we'll be you know  doing the things we really want to do  you know  hug our family and and and you know  recreate and shop and  you know and and see our football games  well that's a great note to end on  [Music]  professor  [Music]  you

